WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.291
Authentication identifies a caller.

2
00:00:02.291 --> 00:00:05.303
Authorization decides what that caller may do.

3
00:00:05.303 --> 00:00:09.296
A common anti-pattern is treating those as the same decision.

4
00:00:09.296 --> 00:00:20.163
A logged-in user is not automatically allowed to view every record, change every setting, export every report, or perform every action reachable from the application.

5
00:00:20.163 --> 00:00:22.978
UI hiding is not server-side authorization.

6
00:00:22.978 --> 00:00:36.856
Hidden buttons, disabled fields, route names, JavaScript checks, obscured links, and frontend state can improve user experience, but they do not enforce access at the point where data is read or state is changed.

7
00:00:36.856 --> 00:00:40.784
The server must make the decision using trusted information.

8
00:00:40.784 --> 00:00:48.640
Client-supplied user IDs, account IDs, tenant IDs, object IDs, roles, scopes, and flags are not authority by themselves.

9
00:00:48.640 --> 00:01:00.554
They are input that must be checked against authenticated identity, server-side state, resource ownership, tenant membership, subscription status, record status, and business policy.

10
00:01:00.554 --> 00:01:06.773
Resource-based authorization is often required when access depends on the object being touched.

11
00:01:06.773 --> 00:01:10.374
The question is not only whether the caller has a role.

12
00:01:10.374 --> 00:01:23.205
It may be whether the caller owns this record, belongs to this tenant, is assigned to this case, has approval for this transaction, or is allowed to act while the workflow is in its current state.

13
00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:31.584
Administrative, export, financial, destructive, configuration, support, and impersonation workflows need explicit policy review.

14
00:01:31.584 --> 00:01:40.618
These paths are often less frequently used than ordinary user workflows, which makes accidental gaps easier to miss during manual testing.

15
00:01:40.618 --> 00:01:46.444
A safer habit is to put authorization near the server-side action and make it reviewable.

16
00:01:46.444 --> 00:01:55.937
Controllers, handlers, services, policies, resource checks, and data filters should show where the decision happens and what information it uses.

17
00:01:55.937 --> 00:02:00.847
Reviewers should not have to trust that the UI prevented an unsafe request.
